       "text": "---\ntitle: How are saved searches and user libraries managed in ADS? Where is this data stored and how is it
     accessed?\nshare_url: https://demo.sourcegraph.com/deepsearch/73a33864-65a4-4985-86d8-8e9d9397f01f\ncreated:
     2026-06-06T07:56:02Z\nupdated: 2026-06-06T07:56:47Z\n---\n\n# How are saved searches and user libraries managed in ADS? Where is this
     data stored and how is it accessed?\n\nI now have a thorough picture of how everything is organized. Here is a comprehensive
     breakdown:\n\n---\n\n## Saved Searches and User Libraries in ADS\n\nADS manages these two features through **two separate
     microservices**, each backed by its own PostgreSQL database.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Saved Searches (myADS Notifications) - `vault`
     service\n\n**Repository:** [github.com/adsabs/vault](https://demo.sourcegraph.com/r/github.com/adsabs/vault)\n\n#### Data
     Storage\n\nThe `vault` service uses PostgreSQL with three core tables defined in
     [vault_service/models.py](https://demo.sourcegraph.com/r/github.com/adsabs/vault/-/blob/vault_service/models.py):\n\n| Table | Purpose
     |\n|---|---|\n| `users` | Maps ADS user IDs to `user_data` (a JSONB blob of preferences) and an optional `library_id` for an OpenURL
     link server |\n| `queries` | Stores serialized Solr queries as binary blobs, keyed by an MD5 hash (`qid`) of the user ID + query body
     |\n| `myads` | Stores per-user notification setups, referencing a `Query` row or standing alone for template-based alerts |\n\nThe
     `myads` table ([models.py L75-91](https://demo.sourcegraph.com/r/github.com/adsabs/vault/-/blob/vault_service/models.py?L75-L91)) has
     these key columns:\n- `type`: either `'query'` (raw saved search) or `'template'` (structured alert)\n- `template`: one of `arxiv`,
     `citations`, `authors`, `keyword`\n- `frequency`: `'daily'` or `'weekly'`\n- `stateful`: whether it tracks new results since last
     check\n- `classes`: arXiv subject classes (for `arxiv` template)\n- `data`: keyword/author string\n- `active`: toggle for
     enabling/disabling\n\n#### API Endpoints
     ([vault_service/views/user.py](https://demo.sourcegraph.com/r/github.com/adsabs/vault/-/blob/vault_service/views/user.py))\n\n| Method
     | Route | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| `POST` | `/query` | Store a Solr query, get a `qid` back (MD5 fingerprint) |\n| `GET` |
     `/query/<qid>` | Retrieve a stored query by ID |\n| `GET/PUT/DELETE` | `/notifications/<myads_id>` | Manage a single notification
     setup |\n| `GET/POST` | `/notifications` | List or create notification setups |\n| `GET` | `/notification_query/<myads_id>` | Build
     the live executable Solr query for a setup |\n| `GET` | `/get-myads/<user_id>` | Fetch full myADS profile for the pipeline |\n| `PUT`
     | `/myads-status-update/<user_id>` | Bulk enable/disable all notifications |\n| `GET` | `/myads-import` | Import classic ADS
     notifications from `harbour-service` |\n\n#### Query Storage Logic\n\nWhen a query is saved ([user.py
     L56-90](https://demo.sourcegraph.com/r/github.com/adsabs/vault/-/blob/vault_service/views/user.py?L56-L90)), the service:\n1.
     Serializes the Solr query to JSON\n2. Computes `md5(user_id_bytes + query_bytes)` as a stable `qid`\n3. Validates the query against
     Solr before storing\n4. Stores the result count at save time (`numfound`)\n\nThis provides **infinite cache-like deduplication** — the
     same query from the same user is never stored twice.\n\n#### Template Query Construction\n\nFor template-type notifications, queries
     are **constructed at execution time** (not stored verbatim). The `_create_myads_query` function ([user.py
     L654-766](https://demo.sourcegraph.com/r/github.com/adsabs/vault/-/blob/vault_service/views/user.py?L654-L766)) builds Solr query
     strings dynamically based on the template type, inserting date ranges relative to \"today\":\n\n- **arxiv**: filters by
     `bibstem:arxiv`, `arxiv_class`, and `entdate` range; daily Mondays get a 2-day look-back\n- **citations**: wraps the saved identifier
     in `citations(...)` \n- **authors**: applies entry date windows to a stored author query\n- **keyword**: generates three separate
     queries — most recent, trending (`trending(...)`), and most cited (`useful(...)`)\n\n#### User Preferences\n\nGeneral user preferences
     (UI settings, link server selection) are stored in the `users.user_data` JSONB column and accessed via [`GET/POST
     /user-data`](https://demo.sourcegraph.com/r/github.com/adsabs/vault/-/blob/vault_service/views/user.py?L141-L215). There are limits
     enforced server-side on key count and value length.\n\n---\n\n### 2. User Libraries (Paper Collections) -
     `biblib-service`\n\n**Repository:**
     [github.com/adsabs/biblib-service](https://demo.sourcegraph.com/r/github.com/adsabs/biblib-service)\n\n#### Data Storage\n\nDefined in
     [biblib/models.py](https://demo.sourcegraph.com/r/github.com/adsabs/biblib-service/-/blob/biblib/models.py), also PostgreSQL:\n\n|
     Table | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `user` | Maps ADS absolute UIDs to local IDs |\n| `library` | One row per library: name, description,
     public/private flag, and `bibcode` (a JSON dict mapping bibcode → `{timestamp}`) |\n| `permissions` | Join table connecting users and
     libraries with `{read, write, admin, owner}` booleans |\n| `notes` | Per-bibcode notes attached to a library; versioned via
     `sqlalchemy-continuum` |\n\nKey design decisions:\n- Library IDs are **UUIDs** ([models.py
     L243](https://demo.sourcegraph.com/r/github.com/adsabs/biblib-service/-/blob/biblib/models.py?L243))\n- Bibcodes are stored as a
     **JSON dict keyed by bibcode**, with the timestamp of when each paper was added ([models.py
     L247](https://demo.sourcegraph.com/r/github.com/adsabs/biblib-service/-/blob/biblib/models.py?L247))\n- Both `library` and `notes`
     tables are **versioned** (change-tracked) via `sqlalchemy-continuum`\n- Permissions cascade-delete when a library is removed\n\n####
     API Structure ([biblib/views/](https://demo.sourcegraph.com/r/github.com/adsabs/biblib-service/-/tree/biblib/views))\n\nViews are
     split across focused modules:\n\n| View file | Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| `library_view.py` | Get/add/remove bibcodes in a
     library; update alternate bibcodes |\n| `document_view.py` | Per-document operations |\n| `permission_view.py` | Manage
     read/write/admin/owner permissions for collaborators |\n| `operations_view.py` | Set operations: union, intersection, difference, copy
     |\n| `notes_view.py` | CRUD for per-bibcode notes |\n| `classic_view.py` | Import from ADS Classic |\n| `transfer_view.py` | Transfer
     library ownership |\n| `user_view.py` | List libraries for a user |\n\n---\n\n### How the Two Services Interact\n\n- The `vault`
     service's `User` model has a `library_id` FK referencing a **different** `library` table — this is **not** the biblib library. It's an
     [OpenURL link server registry](https://demo.sourcegraph.com/r/github.com/adsabs/vault/-/blob/vault_service/models.py?L62-L72)
     (institutional library link resolvers), surfaced via the `/configuration/link_servers` endpoint.\n- The actual paper collection
     libraries live entirely in `biblib-service`.\n- The front-end (Bumblebee/Nectar) communicates with both services through the ADS API
     gateway (`adsws`).\n- The myADS pipeline reads from vault's `/get-myads/<user_id>` endpoint to fetch all active notification setups
     for scheduled email digests.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [saved searches user libraries
